      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im working on a documentary about the decolonization of the belgian congo and i need some help with the research. can u give me a detailed timeline of the events leading up to independence in 1960, but i really want u to focus the narrative on the perspective of the Congolese nationalist movements and the people living under colonial rule? i want to make sure the timeline reflects their struggle and the push for sovereignty.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Elaborated textbook-level solution\n\nCertainly! Here's a concise timeline focusing on the Congolese nationalist movements' perspective leading to independence in 1960:\n\n- 1908: The brutal conquest of the Congo Free State by King Leopold II marks the beginning of intense exploitation and the denial of fundamental rights to the Congolese population, leading to widespread violence and atrocities.\n- Early 1910s: An estimated one-third of the Congolese population perishes due to forced labor, starvation, and medical neglect.\n- World War I: Belgian criticism of Leopold leads to annexation of the territory by King Albert I, but the Congolese populace continues to endure merciless exploitation.\n- Interwar Years (1920s): Growing Congolese awareness of their plight spurs the creation of nationalist movements, most notably the Union of Professional Africans, asserting their rights and calling for Belgian Congo independence.\n- World War II: During \"free French\" rule, Congolese nationalist leaders, like Patrice Lumumba, garner attention, pushing for the colony's autonomy.\n- 1944: With Patrick Lumumba at the helm, the National Movement of the Congolese People revolutionizes the nationalist movement, leading the struggle for self-governance",
